## Show unique values
Now let's show the unique items in the *Items_Bought* column.

::: {#89d7b30f .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
(df
 .select('Items_Bought')
 .unique()
 )
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```{=html}
<div><style>
.dataframe > thead > tr,
.dataframe > tbody > tr {
  text-align: right;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
}
</style>
<small>shape: (8, 1)</small><table border="1" class="dataframe"><thead><tr><th>Items_Bought</th></tr><tr><td>str</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;Cheese&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Milk&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Apples&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Milk&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Bananas&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Apples&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Bread&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Eggs&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
```
:::
:::


<br>
We know that "   Milk" and "Milk" are the same item, but the computer doesn't realize this. The presence of whitespace makes the values different.

## True unique values
To get the true unique values, we need to remove the whitespace from the values. Polars has a handy function that can help with this known as `strip_chars`.

::: {#3b71c21f .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
(df
 .select('Items_Bought')
 .with_columns(pl.col('Items_Bought').str.strip_chars())
 .unique()
 )
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
```{=html}
<div><style>
.dataframe > thead > tr,
.dataframe > tbody > tr {
  text-align: right;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
}
</style>
<small>shape: (6, 1)</small><table border="1" class="dataframe"><thead><tr><th>Items_Bought</th></tr><tr><td>str</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;Cheese&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Apples&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Milk&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Bananas&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Bread&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Eggs&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
```
:::
:::


<br>
See how the number of values has decreased from 8 to 6? That's because "   Milk" and "Milk" are now counted as the same item.

Whenever you want to count the number of unique values in a column, it's good practice to remove any whitespace. This ensures you get an accurate count of the unique values.

